I've been thinking about doing something like this for a while now.
I haven't got any code ready as of yet, but this is how I'd go about
doing it:  (warning, may be High Bittwiddlish)

-maintain a separate linked list, which will have one entry for
 each floorhole-ceilinghole pair.
-this list needs to be saved along with the user data, and it
 really needs to available at any time during play, so we can't
 save it with the temporary level files.
-each entry in the list has the following information:
   -on what level and on what coords the floorhole is?
   -on what level and on what coords the ceilinghole is?
   -flags, that tell:
      -is the entry stable (both holes exist)?
      -one of the holes is not yet created?
      -one of the holes was destroyed?
-whenever a floorhole is created, add an entry to the list,
 and choose a proper deeper level for the ceiling-hole, and mark
 the ceilinghole as not-yet-created.  (and vice versa)
-when player goes through a hole which exit hasn't been created yet,
 choose a random valid (non-solid) position on the exit level, and
 set the exit as created in the entry.
-whenever a floorhole is destroyed, mark it so in the corresponding
 entry, and when the player next visits the level where the exit
 is, destroy the exit too. (and vice versa)
-migrating objects and monsters might need to track which entry
 they used to migrate.
-when saving bones, do not save the chain in it.
-when loading bones, treat the holes as if they were just created.

There are some things that need to be considered: arboreal levels,
levels with undiggable floors, sokoban, ...

All in all, I think it might be better to extend the code that
handles moving via stairs than write yet another separate method 
to move between levels.

Short assessment: This is a huge patch with lots of little fiddly
bits, so unless the devteam takes it up, I doubt it's going anywhere.

